  "headline": "The LoRA did not beat the open base model (52.8% vs 56.4%, discordant pairs 22 vs 35, McNemar p=0.111, 95% site-bootstrap CI -6.7 to -0.6 points: tests disagree: not called real). It removed the format errors: 0 unparseable replies vs 26 for the base model. On 360 held-out test commands from 8 sites: nearest-to-gaze 26.7%, raw gaze 8.3%, open model with gaze 56.4%, gpt-6-astra 57.2%, open model + LoRA 52.8%. LoRA vs frontier: -4.4 points (95% site-bootstrap CI -8.3 to -1.1, McNemar p=0.0559, 23 vs 39 discordant), tests disagree: not called real. LoRA vs nearest: +26.1 points (95% site-bootstrap CI +20.6 to +31.7, McNemar p=4.15e-16, 118 vs 24 discordant), lora better (real: McNemar and cluster bootstrap agree). gaze+command vs command only (open model): +10.6 points (95% site-bootstrap CI +4.7 to +16.4, McNemar p=1.95e-05, 58 vs 20 discordant), open_base better (real: McNemar and cluster bootstrap agree). frontier + geo prompt vs plain prompt: -2.8 points (95% site-bootstrap CI -4.2 to -0.8, McNemar p=0.0129, 2 vs 12 discordant), frontier better (real: McNemar and cluster bootstrap agree). untrained flash base (glm-5p3-flash, NOT the LoRA's base): 58.9% at $0.65 per 1,000 commands vs gpt-6-astra 57.2% at $12.20; median latency 2.8s vs 1.6s, slower than the frontier model. open base model (glm-5p3): 56.4% at $7.76 per 1,000 commands vs gpt-6-astra 57.2% at $12.20; median latency 4.4s vs 1.6s, slower than the frontier model. LoRA (glm-5p3): 52.8% at $5.79 per 1,000 commands vs gpt-6-astra 57.2% at $12.20; median latency 1.6s vs 1.6s, about the same as the frontier model (LoRA latency was sampled inside a training session, not serving). Not run or incomplete: harness_vote, open_base_geo."
